+# Summary: range matching (`satisfies`, `max_satisfying`)
+
+## What changed
+
+- `depres/ranges.py`: implemented `satisfies()` and `max_satisfying()` per `docs/RANGES.md`.
+  - Ranges are parsed into comparator sets joined by `||`. Each set is tokenized (hyphen ranges, operators separated from their versions by whitespace) and expanded into primitive `(op, Version)` bounds.
+  - Partial versions, x-ranges, `~`, `^`, and hyphen ranges all expand per the doc's tables.
+  - The prerelease rule is applied per set: a prerelease version matches only if some comparator in the same set names the same major.minor.patch with a prerelease tag.
+  - Invalid ranges and invalid versions raise `ValueError`.
+  - `max_satisfying` returns the item exactly as given, or `None`.
+- `tests/test_ranges_contract.py` (new): 14 test methods covering the doc's examples, operator/partial table, hyphen, tilde, caret, `||` sets, prerelease rule, build metadata, invalid input, and `max_satisfying` behavior.
+- No existing test file was modified. `tests/test_ranges.py` and `docs/RANGES.md` are unchanged.
+
+## What I verified
+
+- `python -I -m unittest` (run from the project root, Python 3.14.3) before the change: 8 errors (all `NotImplementedError`).
+- `python -I -m unittest -v` after the change: `Ran 22 tests ... OK` (the 8 original tests plus 14 new ones).
+- Two of my own new test expectations were wrong on first run (`1.2.3-beta.x` is a valid prerelease, and `v1.0.0 || v3` correctly excludes `1.5.0`). I fixed the tests, not the implementation.
+- Spot checks with an inline script: `<*` and `>*` match nothing, `<=*` matches all, `1.x - 2`, `>= 1.0.0-alpha`, `~ 1.2.3` (space after operator), and `max_satisfying` on build-metadata ties all behaved as intended.
+- A file under `tests/` changed on disk during the session. I re-read it and reran the suite against its current contents; it passed.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+
+- I did not run the project's CI config or check it against npm's reference semver test suite. CI invocation is assumed to be `python -m unittest` from the repo root, which is what I ran.
+- `"1.0.0 || "` (trailing empty set) matches everything, following the doc's rule that an empty set matches every version. I did not confirm that matches the intended resolver behavior.
+- Non-string range arguments (e.g. `None`) raise `AttributeError`/`TypeError`, not `ValueError`. The contract only covers invalid strings.
+- Versions that compare equal (e.g. differing only in build metadata) return the first one encountered in `max_satisfying`.
+- Nothing outside `depres/`, `tests/`, and `SUMMARY.md` needed to change, so I did not touch anything else.
